CMIIW, but I think this depends on the number of wands/rings.  
If we have n wands and k rings, the chance for 'at least one 
wand/ring is destroyed' should be 

1/200*[1 - ((4/5)^2 + (1/5)^2*(2/3)^{n+k}  
       + (4/5)*(1/5)*[(2/3)^k + (2/3)^n]].

Maybe I am thinking too complicated, but this is how 
I came to the above figures: 

First, we define some events: 

A = Mjollnir hits you on return. 

B = Mjollnir decides to target rings. 

C = Mjollnir decides to target wands.  

D = Mjollnir destroys a certain ring. 

E = Mjollnir destroys a certain wand.  

F = Mjollnir destroys some ring or wand.  

The point I'm insecure about is whether B and C are independent.  
However, from the quote 

| Mjollnir has a flat 20% chance of attempting
| to blow up rings, and a separate flat 20% wands.
                          ^^^^^^^^
I guess that they are.  

Alright, let's draw a tree.  


                Throw
                /  \ 
               /    \
              A     !A
             / \      \
            /   \     !F
           /     \
          /       \  
         /         \
        B          !B
       / \         / \ 
      /   \       /   \ 
     /     \     /     \
    C      !C   !C      C
   / \     / \   \     / \
  F  !F   F   !F  !F  F  !F 


So, 

P(F) = P(A) * P(F|A)
     = P(A) * (1 - P(!F|A))
     = P(A) * [1 - 
       {P(!B and !C | A) + P(!B and C and !F | A)  
        + P(!C and B and !F | A) + P(B and C and !F | A) }  ]
 
Now, since B,C are assumed independent, 

P(!B and C and !F|A) = P(!B|A) * P(C|A) * P(!F| A and !B and C)
                     = 4/5 * 1/5 * (2/3)^{n}

and so on.  

